// **the lifetime, which is the part that is easy to get wrong.** the hoisted declaration is a local of
// a scope wrapping the *statement*, not of the enclosing frame - so a literal argument inside a loop is
// built and destroyed once per iteration. as a frame local it would have held one live collection per
// iteration until the whole block ended, which is a leak in everything but name
//
// the assertion is structural, in the RAST: `Array<T>`'s destructor frees the buffer without running its
// elements' destructors (notes/stdlib.md records that hole), so there is nothing to print - what there is
// to check is *where* the drop sits, which is inside the loop body ahead of the next statement

function first(Array<int32>& $a) : int32 { return $a[0]; }

int32 $i = 0;
int32 $seen = 0;

while ($i < 3) {
    $seen = $seen + first([$i, 0]);
    $i = $i + 1;
}

echo $seen;
